Action: Mark as candidates, not automatic deletions. Preserve useful experiments and confirm non-code consumers
before removal.

P2 / Existing tests largely mock GameContext / invoke room internals; no browser
acceptance or renderer lifecycle suite. Bundle already exceeds warning budget.

Action: Add targeted network/acceptance tests for release risks, split routes/3D code and measure mobile performance.

Custom rules to preserve

Read GAME_RULES.md and GAME_NOTATION.md before rule work. Verified in the engine/room/tests: 42 cards; ranks 7–K then 3,
2, A; black/red Jokers 17/18; Joker trump mapping; five/seven-card targets; clockwise Hot Potato chaining; 3/5/7 mass
conditions and pair counting; one-to-one defense assignment; table pickup; refill to target; picked-up swap-card
provenance; deck-empty escape. Teams and Horse also exist and must survive presentation changes.

Ambiguities remain explicit: Suhuh implementation versus prose, mass eligibility of low-hand defenders, prototype setup
differences, and the meaning of the opening single-player/no-fixed-hand-size wording. No rule changes are authorized
by stale standard-Durak assumptions.

Follow-up findings during implementation

The baseline above remains historical. Implemented fixes and current validation are in PROJECT_PLAN.md.

• Full-match simulations exposed voluntary-pickup cycles in hard-bot decisions and a last-defender turn pointing at a
    player who had already escaped. Fixed the decision policy and clockwise skip; shared engine rules were retained.

• Browser validation exposed a leading attacker frozen at zero seconds, a duplicate Enter-key selection handler, and
    invitation input that uppercased/truncated case-sensitive room IDs. These have been addressed; tests and browser
    checks are recorded in the plan.

• Rematch/refresh validation exposed a cleared reconnect token never restored for the next match. Active seated-player
    state now persists it again; a real browser rematch followed by refresh recovered the game with one canvas.

• Dependency review found critical/high advisories that were not part of the initial test/build audit. Compatible updates
    removed all four critical findings. Remaining advisories and migration limits are documented in

