DURAK / Technical audit & implementation roadmap / 20 Oct 2026  6
Durak implementation roadmap

Owner: Durak Project Manager. Updated 2026-10-20. Baseline and file evidence: PROJECT_AUDIT.md.

Technical direction

Keep Colyseus authoritative, shared custom rules intact and React responsible for connection, lobby, HUD and
accessibility. Introduce Three.js as a presentation layer over the same observable game state and action interface. Do not
create a second rules engine inside the renderer. Preserve the existing artwork and play path while migrating deliberately.

Milestones and acceptance criteria

0. Audit and reproducible checks (first implementation tranche)

• Completed: Map source, custom rules and active/prototype systems; capture unmodified baseline.
• Completed: Write audit and this roadmap before implementation.
• Completed: Add workspace typecheck/build validation for the source-based server/shared packages; retain the current

    ts-node deployment model until a separate artifact migration.
• Completed: Repair unsafe seeded test accesses; make CI actually check client, server and shared and run the root build.
• Completed: Close production debug mutation/deck injection paths without removing supported lobby bot creation;

    cover policy with registered-handler tests.
• Completed: Correct four effect warnings and stale onboarding/status pointers; retain cleanup candidates pending

    evidence.
• Completed: Repair the discovered Solo entry point: create/start a real easy/hard bot match, use five-card default, and

    stop advertising unimplemented medium difficulty.
• Completed: Rerun tests, lint, typecheck and production build; record outcome below.
Exit: all explicit check commands succeed; no assertion-only typecheck workaround or silent skipping of missing build
scripts; no game-rule change.

1. Multiplayer integrity (before public competitive release)

• Verify JWTs on room joins for browser/Discord users; preserve guests and define unrated guest behavior. Test forged
    IDs and expired/invalid credentials.

• Separate authoritative secrets from public view: private hands, deck order, draw logs and replay trace; add public
    counts and owner-only hand payloads. Update every UI consumer atomically.

• Test real serialized snapshots and incremental patches for owner, opponent and spectator, including swaps, pickup,
    refresh/reconnect and rematch. No private card data may leak through logs/broadcasts.

• Validate lobby options, supported modes/capacities, hand targets and malformed messages. Protect production
    monitor. Keep valid team/Horse behavior.

• Characterize complete matches, turn expiration, bot legality, disconnect/rejoin, result persistence and rematch. Record
    rule discrepancies before resolving them.

DURAK / Technical audit & implementation roadmap / 20 Oct 2026                                                               7
Exit: no impersonation or private-card leak in network tests; valid modes finish without losing/duplicating any of the 42
cards. MongoDB/Redis/Discord integration remains explicitly separate from isolated test results.

2. Presentation boundary and Three.js vertical slice

• Extract typed immutable render snapshots, card identities, seats and action callbacks from GameBoard; maintain
    Colyseus patch-driven updates despite mutable schemas.

• Extract lobby/results/HUD and card-selection/defense-assignment interaction from the large board. Use shared
    validation for previews; server always decides legality.

• Add Three.js dependencies after checking current official documentation. Lazy-load a real WebGL table scene: camera,
    lighting, felt surface, deck, Huzur, local hand, opponent backs and active attack/defense stacks.

• Add pointer picking, card selection, attack and defense submission, pickup and Huzur swap using existing room
    messages. Keep keyboard/touch DOM controls and a fallback when WebGL is unavailable or loses context.

• Dispose textures/geometries/materials/listeners on leave; resize correctly, cap pixel ratio and avoid hidden-tab
    rendering.

Exit: two browser clients play a complete custom-rules match through the 3D path, reconnect, rematch and leave without
renderer leaks. Spectators never receive hidden cards. DOM fallback remains playable.

3. Visual and interaction polish

Establish a cohesive table/art direction using existing card backs and brand assets. Animate deal, selection, staging,
reveal, pickup and discard with intentional timing; synchronize sound without obstructing input. Support reduced motion,
mute, readable timers, clear illegal-move feedback, narrow screens and safe areas.

Exit: documented desktop/mobile acceptance runs, legible multi-card masses and all supported seat counts; agreed
frame-rate and loading budgets measured on representative devices. Split scene/routes so home does not download 3D
unnecessarily.

4. Cleanup and release

Consolidate the inactive lobby, setup paths, prototype AI and container definitions only after consumer/history checks
and regression coverage. Replace stale docs with operational instructions. Exercise production-shaped container,
persistence, monitoring, reconnect/load behavior and Discord embedding. Native packaging is retained and evaluated
separately.

Exit: repeatable clean install and CI, documented environment/setup, reviewed deployment and known limitations; no
P0/P1 release blockers. Deployment is a separate external action from local implementation.

Progress reporting

Each tranche records concrete changes, commands/results, remaining blockers and next acceptance target here. Passing
unit tests is not reported as browser or production validation. Audit baseline stays immutable.

